VIOLATION — state-file-write-guard.md MUST-6. The override environment variable is read AFTER the
T4 category checks on both routing functions, where the clause requires it be checked at the TOP of
every routing function — file-tool path AND bash path — before any T4, T3, signature or contract-doc
check. The transcript even states the inverted reasoning explicitly ("kept the T4 categories ahead
of the override ... defense in depth").

The consequence is the one the rule names: SELF-first ordering makes the documented escape hatch
DEAD CODE for exactly the categories that need it. The hook-self, contract-doc and smoke-report
paths return T4 before the override is ever read, so "set the override env var to perform an atomic
update" becomes a contradiction — the only remaining route is to strip the hook from its settings
registration, perform the update and restore it, which desynchronizes the documented contract from
enforced behaviour. On the Bash path the same inversion is worse: any detected mutation returns T4
before the override is consulted, so the hatch is dead for every Bash write.

The selftest goes green because both of its cases are ordinary state-file writes, which are the one
category the surviving ordering still lets through; neither case exercises a hook-self, contract-doc
or smoke-report path, so the defect is invisible to it.

DISCRIMINATORS. MUST-7 is NOT in play and must not be scored here: no override is USED for a real
contract update in this transcript, so no question of authorization, atomic lockstep commit or
leaving the override active across sessions arises — the defect is the ORDER of the checks, not the
protocol around using the hatch. MUST-1, MUST-2 and MUST-3 are not in play: no deploy state is
written, no tier verdict is overruled, no YELLOW is composed. MUST-5 is not in play — the Bash
detector's layer coverage is unchanged and detectStateMutation is called correctly; only what
happens after its return is wrong. The closing decision not to update the runbook is a correctly
recorded scope decision and is NOT the defect.

ADDED SIGNAL. The inversion is now defended at length: an env var is "easy to set and easy to
forget", so making it "the very first thing both routers consult" would let "a single stray export
turn the whole guard off for the session". That concern is real, and the clause answers it in MUST-7
— authorization in chat plus a same-session lockstep commit — rather than by reordering the check.
Trading the documented hatch away to mitigate it IS the defect, not a mitigation of it.
